  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/81/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_14.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The mysterious Green Goblin mounts his \"broomstick\" and heads out to meet the Enforcers (see [LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05]).&nbsp; He promises they'll get their revenge on Spider-Man if they listen to him.&nbsp; He pitches a movie to a Hollywood movie producer, featuring Spider-Man facing off against himself and the Enforcers.&nbsp; It can't lose!\n\nSoon, after school, Liz Allan is all over Peter for demonstrating his brains in class, much to Flash's chagrin, but a \"green-garbed figure on a broom stick\" is spotted over Manhattan, and Spider-Man goes out to investigate.&nbsp; But it turns out that all the Goblin wants is to steer Spider-Man toward the movie producer, who offers him \"fifty thousand dollars\" to star in a movie about him vs. the Enforcers and the Green Goblin.\n\nPeter Parker is even more thrilled when JJJ decides to send <i>him</i> out to take pictures of the movie being made.&nbsp; But Betty thinks he's only interested in the Hollywood starlets, not to mention Liz Allan - she's mad at him.&nbsp; But his money problems will be <i>solved</i>, and he gets permission to go from Aunt May.\n\nJust before the \"big fight scene\" in a deserted area in New Mexico, Spider-Man marvels at how the actors look just like the <i>real</i> Enforcers.&nbsp; Under the guise of wanting to rehearse for their scene, the four of them attack (only then does Spider-Man's spider sense kick in - a little <i>late</i>).&nbsp; Four pages of fighting ensues, with Spider-Man correctly guessing that the Green Goblin is the brains of the operation.&nbsp; (We're given a one-page scene shift back home to peek in on Aunt May, the kids at school and the Daily Bugle though.)\n\nSoon, the battle takes them into a cave (<i>uh oh</i>), where the Green Goblin has them seal the entrance shut so Spider-Man can't get out, and then they pursue him into the cave.&nbsp; More fighting ensues, with the Goblin showcasing the various gadgets he has.&nbsp; But suddenly, from out of the billowing smoke clouds of one of the explosions walks the <i>Hulk</i>!&nbsp; They've stumbled into <i>his</i> cave, and he's none too happy about it, thinking they've come to capture him.\n\nThe Hulk and Spider-Man engage, while the Green Goblin watches gleefully on.&nbsp; The Hulk won't listen to reason, so the now-angry Spider-Man punches him as hard as he can, but the Hulk merely shrugs it off!&nbsp; Spider-Man has no choice but to maneuver himself in front of the boulder that blocks the cave and trick the Hulk into smashing it so he can get out.\n\nBut the Goblin too flies out, and Spider-Man grabs hold of him, and they fly up.&nbsp; But Spider-Man knows he's tired and has no chance of battling him now, so he drops off and even re-enters the cave to haul out the Enforcers so that they don't get beaten to a pulp by the Hulk.&nbsp; He turns them over to the police.\n\nBack at the studio, Spider-Man is paid only for his expenses.&nbsp; He saves a little by taking a bus back to New York instead of flying.&nbsp; He'll be able to give the rest to Aunt May, but in the meantime, the Goblin has returned to NYC.&nbsp; He regrets not realizing that the Hulk was in the area where he intended to battle Spider-Man to the finish so that he could \"organize a world-wide crime syndicate.\"&nbsp; But his identity is still a secret, leaving him free to await his next opportunity.\n\nPeter finally reaches NYC himself, knowing that the menace of the Green Goblin is still out there!",
  "grade_one_liner": "Write something",
  "subtitle": "Write something",
  "review_text": "for its day, the novelty of a villain whose identity was not known to the reader, was a <i>huge</i> innovation in comic book story-telling, so the Green Goblin, as weird as he looked, must have taken the fans by storm (we'll soon find out in the letters pages).&nbsp; You gotta love the final scene of him, unmasked, but with his head obscured by a piece of machinery.\n\nThe fact that the Hulk appears only when the story allows was also quite solid.&nbsp; His appearance did not feel forced in the slightest, so I applaud the creators for using him sensibly and not dragging out his scenes.&nbsp; He essentially drives out the irritating intruders and then returns to his cave - makes perfect sense.&nbsp; It's made quite clear during the course of their brief battle that the Hulk is <i>far</i> stronger than Spider-Man (as if we didn't know that already).\n\nAs for the story and the characters, Spider-Man agreed <i>way</i> too quickly to the movie producer without any attempt at research, and I don't really buy that his spider sense didn't at least give him a tingle at some point <i>before</i> he flew all the way out west to the movie studio and then out to the scene of the \"big fight.\"&nbsp; The Enforcers once again played their roles as \"henchmen\" (this word actually appeared in the book somewhere) - they don't pose a genuine threat to Spider-Man in their own right, but they did at least tire him so that the Green Goblin could pick and choose his spots.&nbsp; The Goblin has the broomstick and various gadgets he employs, and his look really is quite menacing, so I'm sure he was a hit with the readers of the day.\n\nThis story only pays homage to the supporting cast - just enough so we don't forget about them, but they really play no significant role here, and the one-page interlude felt more forced to me than required.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis story which is itself only average, but the intriguing and mysterious new villain and brief guest appearance by the Hulk help tip the scale to upper-mid-grade - solid, but not outstanding, but <i>definitely</i> leaving us with the knowledge that we haven't heard the last of the Green Goblin!\n\nThe <i>Announcements </i>section of the issue had me laughing out loud when they said (in response to the repeated criticism about tooting their own horns): \"We'll try to refer to the following mags (on sale now) <b>without bragging</b>\" and proceeded to do so by using phrases such as \"somewhat pleasant...adequately written...reasonably nice...fair degree of action...you <i>may</i> find it an entertaining issue.\"&nbsp; Concluding with \"Modest enough for you?&nbsp; Take it from us - it isn't easy!&nbsp; Not when we feel like shouting from the rooftops about our mags!\"\n\nThey end with \"So be with us next ish - even if it's just to see if we finally fall on our faces!\"&nbsp; <i>Hahahahaha</i><i>!</i>\n\nI have to say, even though I've read this sort of stuff for decades, putting myself in the heads of the readers of the 60's, this must have been <i>such</i> a breath of fresh air!&nbsp; Stan Lee's genuine and folksy style here really <i>is</i> showcased on a monthly basis, and it's small wonder that as the years went by, and people met him at conventions, that they felt like they <i>knew</i> him - like an old friend.&nbsp; Can you really blame them?",
  "highlight": "Write something"
}